From e115673fa6328454b19036e3ddf93a5ffd08c5ee Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: permissionBRICK <40219477+permissionBRICK@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 16:10:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(face): a recognition threshold per camera; both cameras
 must see one face

The colour and the IR camera's pictures score differently against their
own enrolled faces, but one recognition.threshold served both.
recognition.ir_threshold (default null: the threshold) is the IR
camera's wherever it recognises: as the main camera, and with
anti_spoofing.ir_recognition. Matching compares each face against its
camera's threshold (FaceAuth::recognitionThreshold) instead of the
recognizer's single one; the logs name the camera and its threshold
(also "IR camera" when the IR camera is the main camera).

With ir_recognition the two cameras could recognise two different faces:
a photo on a phone held next to the user's face matched on the colour
camera while the IR camera, which sees nothing on a phone, recognised
the real face beside it. The faces must now be one face: their boxes,
as fractions of each picture, at most anti_spoofing.pair_max_offset
face sizes apart (default 0.5, 0 = no check) and their heights at most
pair_max_size_ratio apart (default 1.5); else the attempt fails like a
spoof (face/common/face_pair.h, checked before the second camera's
match). The cameras sit side by side: on the ASUS Vivobook S 15 the same
face measured 0.95 of its colour width and 0.86 of its colour height in
IR over 5 enrolment pairs, well inside the tolerance. Out-of-range
values turn face off, as thresholds do.

The anti-spoofing model's score and threshold are logged when it passes
too, not only for a spoof.
Tests: face_pair (new), face_both_cameras, config_validation,
config_golden.
